From e2c1a247f803fd0efe83d96ddee63eeccc439a58 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: HDR at more than 8 bits on NVIDIA decided by
 nvidia-modeset from what NVKMS chose, with RGB at 12 bits as a setting of its
 own

Issue 4 (a Quadro RTX 4000 laptop, Turing, whose HDMI port is a dual-mode DisplayPort output without
FRL, and a TV that declares FRL): "HDR at more than 8 bits" sent RGB at 12 bits at 3840x2160 and
23.976 Hz, TMDS at 445.5 MHz, which the TV locked onto and did not show; the answer promised YCbCr
4:2:2 at 12 bits, at 297 MHz. The rule of Kodi for 4:2:2 (OUTPUT_FORMAT::Hdr422Wanted(), through
NVIDIA's NV_CONNECTOR_COLOR_FORMAT) could not give it there: it asked only above 480 MHz and only where
the display declares no FRL, though the link has none where the GPU or the connector has none; it
guessed the TMDS limits of the port and of the display (600 MHz); nvidia-drm has no colour format on a
dual-mode DisplayPort connector; and NVKMS refuses YCbCr422 on Turing (ChooseColorBpc() refuses 10 bpc
on HDMI where the GPU has no 10 bpc over it, and 10 is the depth it gives 4:2:2).

nvidia-modeset now makes the choice in the mode set, from what NVKMS chose for the mode, which holds all
of that (nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9063: hades_hdr_ycbcr422, the parameter of driver 580 with the same
meaning; YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bits where NVKMS chose RGB at 8 bits over TMDS for HDR). So Kodi only writes
the parameters before the mode set of an HDR video, as it did (HdrMoreBits.h):
- "HDR at more than 8 bits where the card sends RGB at 8 (NVIDIA)" (videoscreen.hadeshdrmorebits):
  hades_hdr_ycbcr422, on GeForce GTX 10 and TITAN V (driver 580, 4:2:2 at 10 bits) as before, and now on
  driver 620 too: GeForce GTX 16 and RTX 20 in every mode where RGB stays at 8 bits, and newer cards at
  3840x2160 and 50 or 60 Hz without an HDMI 2.1 link.
- "RGB at 12 bits first (GeForce GTX 16, RTX 20)" (videoscreen.hadeshdrdeepcolour, new, off, under the
  first, shown where the driver has hades_turing_12bpc): what the first setting turned on until now,
  RGB at 12 bits where the display takes 36-bit deep colour and the mode fits, before 4:2:2. It runs the
  link at 1.5 times the clock, and the display of issue 4 locked onto it and showed nothing: a choice
  of the user, not a part of the first setting.
The levels are kept per mode: a mode can be given a lower one than the settings (the step back of the
next change). The colour format request of Kodi for HDR (CWinSystemGbm::AddHdr422(),
OUTPUT_FORMAT::Hdr422Input and Hdr422Wanted()) and CDisplayInfo::SupportsHdmiFrl(), read only for it,
are taken out: the same choice comes from the driver, at 12 bits where NVKMS gave 10. The colour format
of the user (videoscreen.hadescolorformat) and "HDR output depth" of 8 bits fix the format or the depth,
and the driver then leaves both as they are.

Not changed: SDR; native Dolby Vision (8 bits through "max bpc", its own colorimetry); Intel and AMD
(their HDR over the tunnel); every NVIDIA output where NVKMS chose more than 8 bits (RGB at 10 bits over
TMDS or FRL). Host test of HdrMoreBits.h: 57 checks, 17 mutations caught; the files that the change
touches and those that include its headers compiled with the commands of the build (80 of 80). Not tried
on hardware.
